<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koszalin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Köslin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koszalin, in German Köslin, is a city in West Pomeranian Voivodeship, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the Baltic coast. Granted town rights in 1266, it was a main residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamien-pomorski">Cammin</a>, who ruled it as part of their secular territory. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> in the 17th century and to Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koszalin.jpg" alt="Koszalin" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcin.pw · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koszalin_Rynek_Staromiejski_w_2014_roku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Koszalin lies in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in West Pomerania, 12 kilometres south of the Baltic coast, on the river Dzierżęcinka. Founded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was a seaport until the 17th century and one of the main cities of Central Pomerania, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg">Kołobrzeg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Słupsk</a>. Archaeological excavations have found traces of settlement from the Funnelbeaker, Globular Amphora and Lusatian cultures and from Roman times and the early Middle Ages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area became part of the emerging Polish state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> around 967. In 1214 Duke Bogislaw II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> gave the village of Koszalice to a Norbertine monastery, and settlers, mostly German, were invited in. In 1248 the eastern part of Kołobrzeg Land, with the village, passed to the Diocese of Kammin. On 23 May 1266 Bishop Hermann von Gleichen granted it a charter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law, local government and privileges to attract settlers from the west. When the bishops moved their residence to Kołobrzeg in 1276, the diocese was administered from Koszalin, and a Cistercian monastery was founded in 1278.</p><p>The city gained direct access to the Baltic in the 14th century and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in 1386, which brought conflicts with the rival seaports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg">Kołobrzeg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Darłowo</a>. In 1475 a quarrel with Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x">Bogislaw X</a> led to the duke's kidnapping and brief imprisonment in the city. The town became mostly German-speaking, and in 1516 a ban on buying from Slavic speakers and on admitting them to craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> was enforced. In 1534 it became mostly Lutheran under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen">Johannes Bugenhagen</a>.</p><p>Swedish troops occupied Koszalin in 1637 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and after the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> in 1648 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stettin-1653">Treaty of Stettin</a> in 1653 it went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> as Cöslin. A fire in 1718 heavily damaged it. It entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871, and a railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> to Danzig reached it by 1878. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured the city on 4 March 1945, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a> it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Its German population still there was expelled, and the city was resettled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Kashubians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Koszalin is the capital of Koszalin County in West Pomeranian Voivodeship, and was the capital of Koszalin Voivodeship from 1950 to 1998. Its distinctive landmark is the Gothic St Mary's Cathedral from the early 14th century, now the seat of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Koszalin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg">Kołobrzeg</a>. Pope John Paul II visited in 1991 and consecrated the sanctuary on Chełmska Hill. The city has held an annual festival of film debuts since 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koszalin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1248 the bishops acquired the land of Kolberg, forming their secular territory with <b>Köslin</b> and Bublitz.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-cammin">Prince-Bishopric of Cammin</a></li></ul></section>
